{"id":"856ad747-bb84-47ec-8321-d8c328320246","object":"chat.completion","created":1785002892,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        node_by_id[node['id']] = node\\n    root_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None]\\n    if len(root_ids) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Must have exactly one root\\\")\\n    root_id = root_ids[0]\\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_by_id}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        pid = node['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(node['id'])\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if not node.get('removable', False):\\n            continue\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        lose_required = False\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            found = False\\n            for rid in retained:\\n                if rid == nid:\\n                    continue\\n                if node_by_id[rid]['symbol'] == sym:\\n                    found = True\\n                    break\\n            if not found:\\n                lose_required = True\\n                break\\n        if lose_required:\\n            continue\\n        retained.discard(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': sorted_retained}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to generate a Python implementation for the solve(case) function that performs \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" given the payload with nodes and required_symbols. The task is from the interface: we have a list of nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. We need to remove removable nodes while preserving required_symbols and ensuring that the tree remains grammar-valid? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" and \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar\". Likely this is a simplification process: we want to remove nodes that are removable, but we must keep required symbols present. The \"grammar-valid\" likely means that when we remove a node, we need to ensure that its parent still has some child with the required symbols? Or perhaps we need to ensure that after removal, the set of symbols still covers the required symbols? Also we must consider children: if a node is removed, its children are also removed? The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That suggests we should not remove a node if it has children that are retained (i.e., we are keeping them). So removal must be of leaf nodes or nodes whose children are all removed? The algorithm: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes in descending order of depth. For each node that is removable, we check if all required symbols remain represented after removal (i.e., the set of symbols of retained nodes still includes all required_symbols). Also we must ensure that the node has no retained children (since those children would be lost if we remove the parent). But if a node has children that we already decided to remove, then it's okay to remove it. So we need to decide which nodes to remove, in order.\n\nThe output: removed_node_ids (list of node ids in the order they were removed) and retained_node_ids (list of node ids that are kept). The order of retained_node_ids? It says \"Return sorted retained IDs\". So we sort them. The removed order is the actual removal order (presumably the order we decide removal, i.e., the order we process and accept removal).\n\nWe need to implement a function that processes nodes by depth descending, and for each node, if it is removable and not the root, and if removing it does not cause any required symbol to be missing, and if it has no children that are retained (i.e., all its children are either already removed or also being considered), then we can remove it. But we need to be careful: the condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" implies that even if the node is removable, we cannot remove it if it has any child that we have decided to retain. So we need to track which nodes are retained.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Build a mapping from node id to node object.\n- Build a mapping from parent id to list of child ids.\n- Identify root (parent null).\n- Initialize retained set with all nodes? Or initially, we consider all nodes as retained, and we will remove some. The process: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we start with all nodes retained, then we attempt to remove nodes one by one, checking constraints. At each step, we consider the candidate removal set. The order: deeper nodes first, tie-break by node ID (stable, presumably ascending? \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sort nodes by depth descending, then by node ID ascending.\n\nBut we must also consider that removal of a parent may affect children: if parent is removed, children are also removed? The description: \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\" typically replaces a node with its children? Actually Perses removes nodes and replaces with their subtrees? The problem says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" and \"removable\" attribute. The nodes have \"removable\": true/false. For removable nodes, we can choose to delete them and replace with their children? That would be \"replacement\". But the output schema asks only for removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. It doesn't mention replacement order. Perhaps the task is simply to prune nodes that are removable if they don't affect required symbols and if they have no retained children, and we remove them entirely (including any subtree?). But then children of a removed node would also be removed because they are not retained. That matches \"node with retained children\" - if a node has a child that we plan to retain, we can't remove the parent. So we can only remove a node if all its children are either removable and also being removed, or if it has no children. This is consistent.\n\nThus the process: start with all nodes as retained. Iterate over nodes in the specified order (depth descending, tie-break by id). For each node:\n- If not removable -> skip.\n- If root (parent is null) -> skip (root is not removable anyway).\n- Check if node has any child that is currently retained. If yes, skip (cannot remove because would lose retained children).\n- Check if removing this node would cause any required symbol to be missing. To do that, we need to see the set of symbols of all retained nodes if we remove this node. But we are tentatively removing it, so we compute the resulting retained symbols. The required_symbols must be a subset of the symbols of the retained nodes after removal. Since we haven't removed any nodes yet, we can compute: current retained set includes this node. If we remove it, we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there exists at least one retained node (other than this node) with that symbol. So we need to check if the symbol of this node is required and if this is the only node with that symbol. But also removing this node may remove its children? Actually if we remove a node, we also remove its entire subtree. But the algorithm: we are removing nodes one by one. If we remove a parent, we should also remove its children because they are no longer reachable? However, the problem says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That implies that we consider removing a node only if its children are not retained (i.e., we are also planning to remove them). So in the iterative process, when we consider a node, we must ensure that all its children are either already removed or will be removed. But since we process deeper nodes first, children (deeper) are processed before parents. So by the time we get to a parent node, its children have already been considered. So we can mark them as removed/retained. Then for the parent, we check that none of its children are currently retained (i.e., all children are already in removed set). If all children are removed, then removing the parent is allowed, provided required symbols are still present. This matches the algorithm: process deepest first, if a child is removable and we decide to remove it, it gets removed. Then later its parent may be considered. This is akin to bottom-up pruning.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Parse nodes into dict by id.\n- Build children lists.\n- Determine order: sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending.\n- Initialize retained set: all node ids initially.\n- Initialize removed set: empty.\n- Iterate nodes in order:\n   - If node id is root -> skip.\n   - If node not removable -> skip. (However, the root is not removable anyway.)\n   - Check if any child of node is still in retained set. If yes, skip (can't remove parent with retained children).\n   - Compute if removing node would cause loss of required symbol: for each required symbol, check if after removal, there is some retained node (excluding this node) with that symbol. Since we are removing only this node, we need to ensure that for each required symbol, the count of nodes with that symbol in retained set (excluding this node) is at least 1. If the node's symbol is not required, or if other nodes with that symbol remain, it's fine. Also, because children are already removed, they don't contribute. So check: for each symbol in required_symbols, if the only node with that symbol in the current retained set is this node, then can't remove.\n   - If passes, add node id to removed set, remove from retained set.\n- After loop, removed_node_ids is the list of ids in the order they were removed (the order we processed and accepted). retained_node_ids is sorted list (ascending? It says \"sorted retained IDs\" but doesn't specify order; typically ascending string sort). We'll sort lexicographically.\n\nWe need to test on provided examples. Let's simulate first case:\n\nNodes: root (depth0, non removable), n1 (depth1, non removable), n2 (depth1, removable), n3 (depth1, non removable), n4 (depth2, removable, parent n2), n5 (depth2, non removable, parent n3), n6 (depth2, removable, parent n1).\n\nRequired symbols: root, s1. So we need at least one node with symbol \"root\" (root itself) and at least one with \"s1\". Nodes with s1: n1 (depth1, non removable) and n4 (depth2, removable, parent n2). So if we remove n4, we still have n1 with s1. Also there is n6 with s0, not required.\n\nOrder by depth descending: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. Tie-break by id: n4, n5, n6. Then depth1: n1, n2, n3. Depth0: root.\n\nProcess:\n- n4: removable? yes. Its children? none (depth2). So check required: after removing n4, retained still have n1 with s1. So remove n4. retained loses n4.\n- n5: removable? false. skip.\n- n6: removable? true. Children none. Required: symbol s0, not required. So ok. Remove n6. retained loses n6.\nNow depth1:\n- n1: not removable, skip.\n- n2: removable true. Children: n4 was already removed. So no retained children. Check required: symbol s2, not required. Remove n2. retained loses n2.\n- n3: not removable.\n- root: skip (or not removable).\nAfter loop, removed = {n4, n6, n2}. retained = {root, n1, n3, n5}. Sorted: n1, n3, n5, root. But wait, n5 is depth2 and non-removable, so it's retained. That yields retained_node_ids sorted: ['n1','n3','n5','root']. removed_node_ids order: ['n4','n6','n2'] (the order we accepted). Let's check if